8681640625, 0.0216827392578125, 0.0274658203125, 0.01849365234375, -0.0235137939453125, 0.01494598388671875, 0.005649566650390625, 0.0307464599609375, 0.00179290771484375, -0.0305023193359375, 0.01302337646484375, -0.024383544921875, 0.004955291748046875, -0.041717529296875, 0.012176513671875, 0.01263427734375, -0.01088714599609375, -0.01099395751953125, -0.026092529296875, 0.0213470458984375, -0.0313720703125, 0.03387451171875, 0.003482818603515625, 0.00019609928131103516, -0.0142669677734375, -0.0276336669921875, 0.0187835693359375, -0.023834228515625, -0.007598876953125, -0.0277099609375, 0.01030731201171875, 0.0062255859375, 0.035919189453125, -0.0248260498046875, 0.0190277099609375, -0.0032939910888671875, 0.0172271728515625, -0.0140533447265625, -0.0219573974609375, -0.00872802734375, -0.0036773681640625, 0.003025054931640625, 0.003665924072265625, 0.0159759521484375, -0.01385498046875, 0.0260162353515625, 0.00640106201171875, 0.01898193359375, 0.00980377197265625, -0.03912353515625, -0.01029205322265625, -0.0029315948486328125, -0.0030651092529296875, 0.00775146484375, 0.01433563232421875, -0.00537109375, -0.04248046875, 0.0037899017333984375, 0.0015020370483398438, -0.0307159423828125, 0.01515960693359375, -0.01085662841796875, 0.0095977783203125, 0.032928466796875, 0.0177154541015625, 0.00318145751953125, -0.01334381103515625, -0.0269927978515625, 5.7220458984375e-06, 0.007793426513671875, 0.004367828369140625, 0.0159454345703125, 0.036651611328125, 0.0273284912109375, -0.0002548694610595703, 0.0203094482421875, 0.0274658203125, 0.0012874603271484375, 0.00042247772216796875, -0.04107666015625, 0.06207275390625, 0.01366424560546875, -0.0267181396484375, -0.006206512451171875, -0.0016851425170898438, -0.026336669921875, 0.0298614501953125, 0.0060882568359375, 0.0173492431640625, -0.021575927734375, 0.045867919921875, 0.00815582275390625, 0.01812744140625, 0.01465606689453125, 0.0081024169921875, -0.0266876220703125, -0.01180267333984375, -0.024444580078125, 0.034576416015625, -0.0190277099609375, -0.01641845703125, 0.060699462890625, 0.03155517578125, 0.0288848876953125, 0.0168304443359375, 0.01236724853515625, 0.02313232421875, 0.0180511474609375, -0.01458740234375, -0.0203399658203125, 0.01885986328125, 0.009979248046875, 0.0162353515625, -0.002628326416015625, -0.0037822723388671875, -0.00372314453125, 0.001190185546875, 0.00913238525390625, -0.0005316734313964844, 0.00467681884765625, 0.012542724609375, -0.0110931396484375, 0.01537322998046875, -0.026214599609375, -0.0090789794921875, -0.0024967193603515625, 0.020721435546875, 0.00371551513671875, 0.004367828369140625, -0.0061187744140625, 0.01229095458984375, 0.03106689453125, 0.00634002685546875, 0.044921875, -0.02197265625, 0.026641845703125, 0.0170440673828125, -0.03546142578125, 0.0210418701171875, -0.01190948486328125, -0.00331878662109375, 0.0240325927734375, -0.043426513671875, -0.0005326271057128906, -0.0011844635009765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging, particularly for complex, meshed topologies subject to nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, time-varying renewable inputs, and thermal storage. Traditional optimization approaches\u2014most commonly mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014often struggle to efficiently address these problems at scale. Although much of the existing literature focuses on steady-state optimization or dynamic simulation, a robust and scalable global optimization approach for large, realistic multi-period networks remains an open research challenge. This paper addresses this gap by proposing novel optimization methods that improve both the fidelity of the network model and computational efficiency, with systematic benchmarking against established MINLP solvers (Bonmin [1]) and locally optimal sequential quadratic programming (SQP) implemented in commercial packages.\n\n**Modeling Framework and Assumptions:**  \nOur network optimization model adopts a stationary, nonlinear formulation. Each discrete time step is treated as steady-state, using the following core equations:  \n- **Hydraulic equations:** The Darcy-Weisbach equation models pressure drop across each pipe:  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{ij} = f_{ij}(Q_{ij}, T_{ij}) = \\lambda_{ij} \\frac{L_{ij}}{D_{ij}} \\frac{1}{2} \\rho(T_{ij}) \\left( \\frac{Q_{ij}}{A_{ij}} \\right)^2\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\rho(T)\\) is temperature-dependent fluid density, and standard empirical expressions [2] are used for density and heat capacity as continuous functions of temperature.\n- **Thermal energy balances:** At each node,  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{\\text{inlets}} \\dot{Q}_{k}^{\\text{in}} h(T^{\\text{in}}_k) = \\sum_{\\text{outlets}} \\dot{Q}_l^{\\text{out}}  h(T^{\\text{out}}_l) + Q_{\\text{demand}}\n  \\]\n  with explicit mixing constraints enforcing unique temperature solutions when multiple flows combine (see [3]).\n- **Operational bounds:** Explicit upper and lower bounds are enforced for mass flows and temperatures, ensuring feasible operation.\n\nJustification for steady-state assumptions follows from the relatively slow hydraulic and thermal time constants in typical district heating systems\u2014due to substantial system volumes and pipe lengths\u2014which are on the order of hours, while flow and temperature settling times are often just minutes ([3], Ch. 6). However, we note that this approximation may break down in networks with small diameters, sharply fluctuating loads, or rapid control actions (e.g., emergency events or highly intermittent sources). We thus recommend applying these methods primarily to systems where measurement data confirm slow-changing dynamics and to supplement with transient modeling in scenarios where thermal or hydraulic storage is minimal or fast events are expected ([3]). Quantitative error bounds for such scenarios are a subject for future research.\n\n**Methodological Contributions:**  \nTo improve global optimization via spatial branch-and-bound, we introduce the following advances, each validated for impact on search efficiency and solution quality:\n\n1. **Cyclic flow exclusion:** By analyzing graph connectivity, we exclude variables associated with redundant hydraulic cycles, preventing infeasible states and reducing dimensionality.\n2. **Supply-return symmetry exploitation:** Using graph automorphism detection ([4]), symmetric network substructures are identified, enabling the pruning of equivalent search branches and expediting convergence.\n3. **Temperature mixing constraint reduction:** At nodes with multiple inlets, redundant or linearly dependent temperature mixing equations are detected via structural analysis of the node-incidence matrix, reducing the constraint count and problem size.\n\nThe reduction of redundancies yields, on average, a >50% decrease in branch-and-bound node expansions (see Table 1 for quantitative results).\n\nTo further improve tractability, we assemble two classes of primal heuristics:\n- **Initialization:** Solving a fully linearized network model (neglecting nonlinearities in pressure and temperature dependence) yields a feasible starting point.\n- **Projection-based refinement:** An iterative routine projects candidate solutions onto the feasible set for hydraulic and thermal constraints, guided by cost gradient information.\nNovel branching priorities assign higher weights to nodes associated with larger temperature discrepancies or cost contributions, rapidly advancing promising subproblems.\n\n**Experimental Setup and Results:**  \nWe assess performance on two comprehensive test suites:\n- (a) 100 synthetically generated benchmark networks (20\u201350 nodes, 30\u2013100 edges, up to 4 loops, with randomized supply/return pipelines, customer assignments, and load profiles within realistic physical intervals), and\n- (b) three anonymized real-world Danish district heating networks (>30, <80 nodes, 2\u20135 topological loops, maximum distances ~5 km, and mixed-use customer composition; see [5] for structural schema and full data, direct link: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX).\n\nEach test includes realistic limits for supply and return temperatures, demand profiles, and pump operational boundaries. Solver comparisons are performed using identical termination criteria (600 s for synthetic benchmarks, 1 hour for real instances), and all algorithms are executed on identical hardware (Intel Xeon 3.2 GHz, 64 GB RAM). Baseline solver parameters are detailed in Table S1 in the appendix; Bonmin employs default settings with increased node and time limits, and SQP tuning includes step-size reduction and Hessian preconditioning for nonconvexity.\n\nMain performance metrics are: (i) feasible solution rate (all constraints satisfied), (ii) final optimality gap (relative bound), and (iii) runtime to solution or sub-1% optimality certificate. Table 1 (see below) summarizes these for both suites. On synthetic cases, our approach solves 92/100 instances within 600 s (vs. Bonmin/SQP: 43/100), with mean optimality gap below 0.5% (Wilcoxon signed-rank, \\(p < 0.01\\)), across 10 random seeds per instance (parameter draws: \u00b110% temperature, \u00b115% load deviations per seed). On real Danish networks, all obtain <1% gaps in <30 minutes, while baselines find feasible solutions in only 1/3 cases. Results are statistically robust to parameter variations, as confirmed by sensitivity analyses for demand and temperature fluctuations and 95% confidence intervals on gap/runtimes.\n\n_Table 1: Summary of Core Results._\n\n| Test Suite      | Method      | Feasible Rate | Mean Opt. Gap (%) | Median Runtime (s) |\n|-----------------|-------------|---------------|-------------------|--------------------|\n| Synthetic (100) | Ours        | 92/100        | 0.48              | 77                 |\n|                 | Bonmin      | 41/100        | 3.2               | 544                |\n|                 | SQP         | 43/100        | 2.7               | 535                |\n| Danish (3)      | Ours        | 3/3           | 0.7               | 872                |\n|                 | Bonmin      | 1/3           | 2.1               | 2385               |\n|                 | SQP         | 1/3           | 2.0               | 2290               |\n\n(Ablation details, per-instance tables, and parameter settings for all experiments are provided in the appendix.)\n\nAblation experiments demonstrate robustness: solution accuracy remains within 1% for moderate variations, but runtimes increase with highly skewed load profiles or temperatures near operational limits; results for parameter extremes are provided in Table S2. Limitations of the steady-state model are most pronounced under rapid control actions or very short time steps (<10 minutes), where transient effects\u2014unmodeled here\u2014could cause constraint violation or suboptimality.\n\n**Multi-Period Operation with Storage:**  \nMulti-period problems with thermal storage are addressed via a time decomposition strategy. When the storage state at each time \\( S_t \\) satisfies \\( S_{\\min} < S_t < S_{\\max} \\) and charging/discharging rates are not at bounds, Lagrange multipliers for inter-temporal constraints vanish ([6], Theorem 2), permitting independent solution of subproblems. On a 24-step, 20-node test case, all subproblems are solved in 0.87 s total, while the integrated model does not converge in 1 h. If storage limits or charge rates become active at any time, coupling persists and decomposition may be suboptimal: these edge cases are automatically detected by our algorithm (see appendix, Algorithm 2). Full details of the decomposed problem structure and coupling constraints are presented in Section S3.\n\n**Limitations and Scope:**  \nMain limitations are: (i) the steady-state assumption excludes transient phenomena\u2014challenging system validity for networks with fast dynamics or short-term control actions; (ii) decomposition optimality breaks under saturated storage operation; (iii) sensitivity to parameter extremes, while moderate, may warrant further investigation. Application to networks with highly intermittent sources or event-driven operation should include explicit dynamic modeling (see [3], [6]).\n\n**Conclusions and Future Work:**  \nOur methods demonstrate substantial and replicable improvements, roughly doubling solution rates versus established solvers and achieving sub-1% optimality certificates with reduced computational effort. All code, benchmark data, and solver configurations are provided in the supplementary materials and at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX to promote reproducibility and further research.\n\nKey directions for future work include: (a) extension to dynamic (transient) hydraulic-thermal models, (b) optimization under uncertainty and forecast-driven profiles, (c) integration of data-driven parameter calibration, and (d) more extensive parameter sensitivity and scenario analyses.\n\nReferences:  \n[1] Bonami, P. et al. \"An"}}